WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:02.030
When we took an oath to do no harm

00:00:02.030 --> 00:00:04.010
that should imply do no financial harm.

00:00:04.010 --> 00:00:05.640
We make it cheaper than a cell phone.

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:07.180
If you can afford a cell phone,

00:00:07.180 --> 00:00:09.356
you can afford the most basic aspect

00:00:09.356 --> 00:00:11.560
of healthcare delivery
in the United States.

00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:13.720
The patients of doctors William Crouch

00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:16.600
and Lee Gross know exactly
what services will cost

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:17.980
before they receive them,

00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:20.410
a radical concept only in healthcare.

00:00:20.410 --> 00:00:21.590
They don't have to deal with

00:00:21.590 --> 00:00:23.820
benefits packages, coverage denials,

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:26.820
hidden costs, in network
versus out of network,

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:28.520
or any surprises whatsoever.

00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:31.140
Instead their patients
buy the medical equivalent

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:32.750
of a Netflix subscription

00:00:32.750 --> 00:00:35.007
We charge $75 a month for an adult,

00:00:35.007 --> 00:00:36.940
$30 a month for one child,

00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:39.330
and $15 a month for each additional child.

00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:40.430
After that recharge nothing

00:00:40.430 --> 00:00:42.870
for the services that are
provided in our office.

00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:45.300
Doctors Crouch and Gross
of North port, Florida

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:47.760
are pioneers in a
growing national movement

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:49.530
called Direct Primary Care.

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:50.900
Physicians around the country

00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:53.150
who are tired of dealing
with insurance companies

00:00:53.150 --> 00:00:55.500
when it comes to routine medical services

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:57.440
have exited the traditional system

00:00:57.440 --> 00:00:59.640
and are saying that they
can provide better care

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:01.910
at a lower price by
charging their patients

00:01:01.910 --> 00:01:04.110
a nominal monthly fee directly.

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:06.700
COVID-19 has pushed many doctor's offices,

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:08.920
which have been hard hit by the pandemic,

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:11.830
to start doing telemedicine
for the first time.

00:01:11.830 --> 00:01:13.840
And insurance companies in the government

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:15.770
have started paying them for this service.

00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:18.250
For now, the direct primary care practices

00:01:18.250 --> 00:01:20.670
have proven far more agile and responsive

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:22.180
to the needs of patients.

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:23.130
They're demonstrating

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:25.980
that making American health
care flexible and affordable

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:28.930
requires abandoning the use
of third-party insurance

00:01:28.930 --> 00:01:32.300
for routine care and adopting
a free market approach.

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:33.430
Direct primary care is about

00:01:33.430 --> 00:01:35.250
as close to a free market in healthcare

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:37.010
as you've ever seen in our country.

00:01:37.010 --> 00:01:38.010
Now, people say that we,

00:01:38.010 --> 00:01:40.590
well we tried free market, it didn't work.

00:01:40.590 --> 00:01:42.270
That's why we need the
government to take over.

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:44.200
That's why we need a single
payer healthcare system.

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:47.260
We have never tried a true
marketplace in healthcare.

00:01:47.260 --> 00:01:49.310
When I was in a fee for service system,

00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:51.370
I felt like I was playing
a game of whack-a-mole

00:01:51.370 --> 00:01:52.203
with Medicare.

00:01:52.203 --> 00:01:54.900
We had to find ways of doing as much stuff

00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:56.200
to as many people as possible

00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:57.690
to generate as much revenue

00:01:57.690 --> 00:01:59.350
just to pay for the computer systems

00:01:59.350 --> 00:02:02.010
that I needed to bill Medicare
so that I could get paid.

00:02:02.010 --> 00:02:05.470
And you kept seeing that
people were being denied care

00:02:05.470 --> 00:02:07.530
and a lot of it was cost prohibitive.

00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:10.110
They were able to afford
their insurance premiums

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:12.170
but then they couldn't
afford the needed test.

00:02:12.170 --> 00:02:14.760
And every time I found a way to prop up

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:18.450
and be able to generate revenue
to support this monstrosity

00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:20.010
that we were required to build,

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:22.030
Medicare would not cut the
knees out from under us

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:23.950
and take away that revenue source

00:02:23.950 --> 00:02:24.983
where eventually we just said,

00:02:24.983 --> 00:02:25.816
You know what?

00:02:25.816 --> 00:02:26.649
No more.

00:02:26.649 --> 00:02:28.140
Direct primary care practices

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:28.990
are demonstrating

00:02:28.990 --> 00:02:30.560
that routine health services

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:32.690
covered by Medicare
and insurance companies

00:02:32.690 --> 00:02:34.580
cost so little that most patients

00:02:34.580 --> 00:02:36.750
could easily afford them out of pocket.

00:02:36.750 --> 00:02:39.570
So how did this third-party
payer system develop?

00:02:39.570 --> 00:02:41.950
The government created it
through the tax system.

00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:45.250
During world war two the IRS
started allowing employers

00:02:45.250 --> 00:02:46.540
to provide health insurance

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:48.810
as a form of pre-tax compensation.

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:51.170
But if employees purchased
their own healthcare

00:02:51.170 --> 00:02:53.620
they had to use after-tax dollars.

00:02:53.620 --> 00:02:56.050
This led to a system in
which insurance companies

00:02:56.050 --> 00:02:57.570
and large healthcare providers

00:02:57.570 --> 00:03:00.190
negotiate prices behind closed doors,

00:03:00.190 --> 00:03:02.130
leaving patients out of the mix.

00:03:02.130 --> 00:03:02.963
What we have done is

00:03:02.963 --> 00:03:05.410
we've essentially disrupted
that entire paradigm.

00:03:05.410 --> 00:03:07.780
Because we've said let's
have price transparency.

00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:09.910
Let's show people what
these services actually cost

00:03:09.910 --> 00:03:11.270
because they do have a dollar value.

00:03:11.270 --> 00:03:12.770
You can put a price tag on these things.

00:03:12.770 --> 00:03:13.603
We've proven that.

00:03:13.603 --> 00:03:15.820
Today there are approximately 1400

00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:18.220
independent direct primary care practices

00:03:18.220 --> 00:03:20.520
with a presence in 49 States.

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:22.870
Virtually all of them
charge a subscription fee

00:03:22.870 --> 00:03:25.320
that's between 50 and 100 dollars monthly

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:27.610
to consult with the doctor at any time

00:03:27.610 --> 00:03:29.460
in person or from home.

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:31.720
Crouch and Gross provide routine services

00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:34.000
like preventative checkups, EKGs,

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:35.960
minor procedures like biopsies,

00:03:35.960 --> 00:03:39.320
joint injections, removal
of cysts and small cancers,

00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:40.470
and some urgent care

00:03:40.470 --> 00:03:42.240
such as sewing up lacerations

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:45.960
and splinting uncomplicated
fractures, no extra charge.

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:48.610
In-office tests like those
for strep and pregnancy

00:03:48.610 --> 00:03:49.920
are included as well.

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:51.930
If a test needs to leave the office

00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:53.510
patients pay cash prices

00:03:53.510 --> 00:03:56.790
that Gross and Crouch have
negotiated on their behalf.

00:03:56.790 --> 00:03:58.390
If you asked the lab, what
they'll tell you is that

00:03:58.390 --> 00:04:01.140
the most expensive thing
that they do is not the lab.

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:02.750
It's the cost of the human labor

00:04:02.750 --> 00:04:05.540
associated with processing
those claims and getting paid.

00:04:05.540 --> 00:04:08.500
So if we eliminate their
number one line item expense

00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:10.330
in their service delivery,

00:04:10.330 --> 00:04:12.040
then we can bring those prices way down.

00:04:12.040 --> 00:04:13.320
And that's exactly what we saw.

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:16.680
We would see 95% discounts
on the laboratory services.

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:19.050
According to a survey conducted in July,

00:04:19.050 --> 00:04:22.820
78% of physicians had seen
a decline in patient volume

00:04:22.820 --> 00:04:24.320
because of COVID 19.

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:27.420
In March, the center for
Medicare and Medicaid services

00:04:27.420 --> 00:04:28.950
issued a temporary waiver

00:04:28.950 --> 00:04:31.170
stating that Medicare
would pay the same rate

00:04:31.170 --> 00:04:33.950
for certain kinds of
video telemedicine visits

00:04:33.950 --> 00:04:35.160
as in-person ones.

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:36.910
But the types of visits that would cover

00:04:36.910 --> 00:04:38.640
changed over the course of the year

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:40.070
and are still changing.

00:04:40.070 --> 00:04:42.380
Whether or not insurance
companies and the government

00:04:42.380 --> 00:04:45.380
continue covering online
visits after the pandemic

00:04:45.380 --> 00:04:48.040
has no bearing on Crouch
and Gross's practice.

00:04:48.040 --> 00:04:49.180
And they didn't have to wait

00:04:49.180 --> 00:04:51.060
for insurance companies and the government

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:53.580
to okay telemedicine in the first place.

00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:55.720
And we immediately flipped the switch,

00:04:55.720 --> 00:04:58.110
and instantly from in-person practice

00:04:58.110 --> 00:04:59.730
we were an online practice.

00:04:59.730 --> 00:05:01.611
We were a parking lot practice.

00:05:01.611 --> 00:05:03.340
We did whatever we had to do

00:05:03.340 --> 00:05:05.400
in order to get the
patient the proper care

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:06.400
at the proper time.

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:09.700
Telemedicine has added complexity
to the billing process.

00:05:09.700 --> 00:05:12.290
That has no impact on Crouch and Gross.

00:05:12.290 --> 00:05:13.470
So we didn't need to wait

00:05:13.470 --> 00:05:15.710
for blue cross to convene a committee

00:05:15.710 --> 00:05:18.010
to pay for telemedicine services.

00:05:18.010 --> 00:05:20.460
I didn't need to wait two
months or three months

00:05:20.460 --> 00:05:22.490
for Medicare to create a new billing code

00:05:22.490 --> 00:05:25.560
in order for me to provide
technology visits for a patient.

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:26.393
I just did it.

00:05:26.393 --> 00:05:29.840
For what Medicare pays for
a single technology visit,

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:31.460
I provide two to three months

00:05:31.460 --> 00:05:33.270
of unlimited technology visits,

00:05:33.270 --> 00:05:36.010
Unlimited office visits,
unlimited home visits,

00:05:36.010 --> 00:05:37.550
unlimited email visits.

00:05:37.550 --> 00:05:41.140
And so the model, is
again, pandemic tested.

00:05:41.140 --> 00:05:43.880
It's proven that it's
actually a superior model

00:05:43.880 --> 00:05:46.600
because we have the built-in
flexibility to do what we need

00:05:46.600 --> 00:05:47.670
at the time we need it.

00:05:47.670 --> 00:05:50.270
32 States in DC have passed laws

00:05:50.270 --> 00:05:51.930
requiring insurance companies

00:05:51.930 --> 00:05:53.200
to reimburse doctors

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:55.590
at the same rate for telemedicine visits

00:05:55.590 --> 00:05:58.100
as they do for comparable
in person visits.

00:05:58.100 --> 00:06:00.430
Crouch and Gross say
that shouldn't be decided

00:06:00.430 --> 00:06:03.550
by lobbyists, lawmakers, or
government administrators.

00:06:03.550 --> 00:06:06.500
Prices should be set
through market competition.

00:06:06.500 --> 00:06:08.400
The very first time I went to Washington

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:10.070
and made a presentation
on direct primary care,

00:06:10.070 --> 00:06:11.450
I gave it to a group of physicians

00:06:11.450 --> 00:06:13.910
and after I gave my
presentation on our practice

00:06:13.910 --> 00:06:15.917
and what we were doing, a
doctor raised his hand and said,

00:06:15.917 --> 00:06:18.457
What happens if some doctor
sets up right next door to you,

00:06:18.457 --> 00:06:20.106
and charges $40 a month?

00:06:20.106 --> 00:06:22.220
And then I said, That's
an excellent question.

00:06:22.220 --> 00:06:24.157
I said, Because of the first
question out of the audience

00:06:24.157 --> 00:06:24.990
is what are we going to do

00:06:24.990 --> 00:06:27.047
when we bring down the
price of healthcare?

00:06:27.047 --> 00:06:28.517
We're onto something because that question

00:06:28.517 --> 00:06:29.350
has never been asked

00:06:29.350 --> 00:06:31.600
in the American healthcare system ever.

00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:32.710
I said, But here's what's gonna happen.

00:06:32.710 --> 00:06:34.647
I said, That doctor and I
are going to have to compete

00:06:34.647 --> 00:06:36.067
on price and quality.

00:06:36.067 --> 00:06:37.477
And I'm gonna have to justify

00:06:37.477 --> 00:06:39.601
why my price is twice as much.

00:06:39.601 --> 00:06:41.297
Maybe I provide better service.

00:06:41.297 --> 00:06:42.727
Maybe I'm just better trained

00:06:42.727 --> 00:06:44.927
have better credentials,
have more experience,

00:06:44.927 --> 00:06:47.877
but something tangible is
going to have to justify that.

00:06:47.877 --> 00:06:49.247
Or I'm gonna have to lower my prices

00:06:49.247 --> 00:06:50.957
and to compete or I'm gonna lose patients

00:06:50.957 --> 00:06:52.430
to the person down the street.

00:06:52.430 --> 00:06:55.150
The myth is that profit
by its mere definition,

00:06:55.150 --> 00:06:57.920
it does not belong in the
American healthcare system

00:06:57.920 --> 00:07:00.680
and it's evil when it
creates perverse incentives.

00:07:00.680 --> 00:07:03.040
The key to making that
profit work is again

00:07:03.040 --> 00:07:04.850
that elimination of that third party

00:07:04.850 --> 00:07:06.050
in the middle of that profit

00:07:06.050 --> 00:07:08.797
which just drives up
cost but adds no value.

